.TH CRYPTOVEC 1
.SH NAME
cryptovec \- apply a keyring primitive to hex inputs, for differential testing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cryptovec
.I primitive inputfile
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Cryptovec
reads hex-encoded inputs, one per line, from
.IR inputfile ,
applies the named
.IR keyring (2)
.I primitive
to each, and writes the hex-encoded result, one per line, to standard
output. It computes nothing itself beyond hex framing \(em the
cryptography is keyring's. It exists so a host-side harness can feed the
same inputs to this tree's crypto and to a formal Cryptol specification
and diff the outputs; see
.B /tests/cryptolverify.sh
and
.IR cryptol (1)
(the Galois specification language).
.PP
Input is read from a file, not standard input, because host-piped
standard input is intercepted by
.IR emu 's
console. The line format depends on the primitive:
.TP
.B sha256 \fR| \fBsha512
a hex message; the output is the digest.
.TP
.B chacha20
a 32-byte key, a 12-byte nonce, a 4-byte little-endian block counter,
then the plaintext, all concatenated in hex; the output is the
ChaCha20 (RFC 8439) keystream XORed with the plaintext.
.TP
.B poly1305
a 32-byte one-time key then the message; the output is the 16-byte tag.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cryptovec.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyring (2),
.IR chachatest (1),
.B /tests/cryptolverify.sh
.SH BUGS
A test aid, not a general tool: the input encodings are fixed to what
the differential harness needs, and only the primitives that harness
checks are implemented.
